TODO items for mcstas-ess-instr-citool
======================================

* runci: MPI support in runtest.py is stubbed out (do_mpi = False, marked
  FIXME). Make it work.

* runci: The JSON test results produced by mctest are only used to check for
  failures which mctest did not report in its exit code (see check_results
  in runtest.py). Decide whether to use them for anything else (e.g.
  summarise, store as CI artifacts, compare between runs).

* generatepy: The instrument renaming and the translation of %Example lines
  into McStasScript tests are done by the tool when mcstas-pygen does not
  support this (McStas 3.8.8). Once a McStas release with these features in
  mcstas-pygen is required, remove the workarounds in generatepy.py.

* instrpy projects with includes/: When using the McStasScript instrument
  directly (rather than via this tool), McStasScript writes and compiles the
  .instr file in its input_path directory, where #include "includes/..."
  only works if includes/ is copied there by hand. Proposed fix: make() adds
  "-I<package dir>" to the DEPENDENCY line (instr.set_dependency), so the C
  compiler finds the includes/ files in the package itself (tested to work
  with ESS03 generated by generatepy). Then generatepy should add this to
  the generated code, and generate/runci should stop copying includes/ for
  instrpy projects, so CI tests what users get.

* %Example lines without any parameters fail in mctest (the instrument then
  asks for the parameter values interactively). An upstream fix for mctest
  has been proposed (McCode branch mctest-example-defaults, which passes -y
  for such lines). Until a McStas release with this fix is required, such
  lines must list at least one parameter (as done by add_example in the
  ESS04 example).
